joining us this morning with me hope  fewer audio problems at the transport  secretary grant chaps mr shaps good to  see you this morning um  somewhat appropriate that we come off  the back of the gdp figures and a week  of big announcements  yours yesterday certainly one that  people are hoping will give a shot in  the arm to the economy just just take us  through the thinking  uh behind that which you announced  yesterday particularly as regards the  the the lack of requirement for people  coming back double vaccinated  from ambulance company countries to  isolate  yeah well that's right i mean up until  now if you've gone to an amber this  country  uh we'll start we said you shouldn't be  going there for holidays but you also  had to come back and you had to  quarantine or self-isolate what we did  yesterday on the basis of the fact that  we now have  such a large proportion of uh of people  um fully vaccinated it said if you have  been fully vaccinated  then you can essentially travel from the  19th of july  to an ambulance country without needing  to come back and quarantine you'll still  need to come back and take a day two  pcr test um but that will of course open  up  large number of countries which fall  into that amber category by far the  biggest group  between the red amber and green so good  news for people who are  looking forward to maybe seeing loved  ones abroad or even catching some sun  but what though of of loved ones who who  live abroad and come back here as you  say you're talking about people who are  who are fully vaccinated but fully  vaccinated  via the nhs i think at the moment there  are around about 30 countries that  accept  our certification why won't we accept  anyone else's  well that would be phase two of it and  something we're very actively working on  at the moment of course with the uk  vaccination program  we're able to you're able to demonstrate  your vaccine status very easily the  full nhs app that's not the covert app i  should  point out is the regular nhs app app  contains your  record for your uh vaccines so once  you've been fully vaccinated it  effectively gives you a certificate  which you can show on screen or download  so it's easy for us to be able to use  that in order to demonstrate the status  for  somebody returning home uh that's the  first stage  the next thing is to be able to  recognize uh apps from  other countries or certification from  other countries easier done from some  places like  the eu where they have a digital um app  coming along  uh than it is in the united states where  they have i think 50 different systems  one for each state  largely paper based so there's some  complexities to work through there  but this is phase one and we hope to  follow it up quickly  with double vaccinated people from other  countries coming here  can you put some sort of time frame on  that because clearly  a lot of people are looking forward to  your announcement yesterday they may  well have some time off coming in this  summer there are an awful lot of expat  brits wanting to come back to the united  kingdom  to see their family do you think you're  going to be able to have this done what  next couple of weeks next couple of  months  by the end of the summer yeah so i mean  first of all this announcement kicks in  from the 19th of july for anyone who's  been vaccinated in the uk and then  secondly we're actively working on this  issue of  um how to accept uh vaccinations from  other people obviously we look at  whether they are uh world health  organizations certified and like  and i would think in terms of time scale  in the next couple of weeks i'll be able  to come forward and say more about other  locations in the world  do you understand though the frustration  of of expatriate brits  for example living in italy this weekend  we're going to see around about a  thousand  italian fans coming to this country to  watch the football  and then heading straight back home  afterwards  there will be thousands of people  looking at that and saying well why  can't we do the same  people who perhaps haven't seen their  families for the best part of a year and  a half  yeah yeah no i mean look we want to be  able to open that up for people and  actively working on it i should say with  the with the football um  they come to a specific location they're  taking direct to the football  uh they leave again 12 hours later there  isn't  any they're unable to travel anywhere  else et cetera it's a very restricted  um program but nonetheless i'm going to  take the point that we do want to  get this done part part of the issue of  course is um elsewhere  the level of fully vaccinated people  tends not to be at the levels that we've  got in the uk  because we got on with our vaccination  program so much faster  so you know there aren't as many fully  vaccinated people  for example from europe to come here but  nonetheless we are working on it we do  want to get  this part of it resolved as well i  thought it was important with this phase  one  section you know people who've been  vaccinated in the uk  to get that information out there as  soon as we were ready to make the  announcement which is why i did that  yesterday  but i said we'll follow up with phase  two for people coming the other way  as soon as we can just explain as well  if you can uh minister the  the rationale for for asking for a pcr  test  from children as young as five six years  of age  yeah so first of all i should point out  the um  children 18 year olds and under oh  under 18 of course children will be able  to follow the same rules  regardless of whether they've been  double vaccinating of course the answer  is they won't have been double  vaccinated because currently  the joint committee on vaccines and  immunization have not recommended yet  vaccinating um children and we're  waiting for them to opine on it  so we thought it was right and proper  because they can't be vaccinated  to allow them to adhere to the same  rules but we must of course make sure  that we've got  protection uh for you know everybody in  the united kingdom in place  and a pcr test remains the best way to  do that and it does mean that we're able  to then  uh keep a very close eye on those  variants of concern  which are of course the thing that we  are most worried about now we don't want  to put  the country at risk and and that could  be carried by uh  a child as well so that's the reason why  we're doing it and this is the reason  why everybody will still have to take a  pcr test on day two  when they return in indeed you mentioned  a couple of minutes ago the  the the the nhs uh covered 19 app and  you'll have seen you'll have  i'm presuming actually i've had  conversations about this with with  people in your own department about the  concerns that  when we get into this summer and we see  as the health secretary outlined cases  perhaps running as high as a hundred  thousand new hundred thousand new  cases a day perhaps even higher than  that that we are going to be in a  position where we are seeing potentially  millions of people  and certainly not the majority of them  with covert 19 but millions of people  being forced  to self-isolate what action is being  taken on the app at the moment  yeah that's right i take advice on this  or i we take advice on this from  um the medical um experts and you know  as things like  um the the the overall nationwide levels  for  things like uh the the amount of  distance that you need to keep the  social distancing and other rules  are looked at so will we look at the way  that the app  performs with regard to what are now the  new standards uh being in place so  always keep a very close eye  on those sort of things i i did um speak  to the health secretary about it  yesterday  he's very aware of this and we'll keep  it under constant review because  we want the app to be a useful um tool  in our armory  uh to make sure that we're all able to  uh be aware of and ultimately defeat the  coronavirus and we want it to keep  continue to work in that way  yep so sorry to interrupt mr we're just  a bit pushed for time but but people are  deleting the app as we speak people are  switching off the contact tracing aspect  of it it is fine that  there will be some tinkering we're told  that it will be less sensitive  uh in future but you know if people have  deleted it from their phone they're  unlikely to be putting it back on it  just feels like we might have missed the  boat in this one  that's not actually the evidence by the  way we're still seeing lots of people  downloading the  um app i've encouraged people to do so  as to say we'll keep the  sensitivity of it under review to align  with the new  um recommendations that come in  guidelines to come in on the 19th of  july so we'll continue to do that it is  very useful tool in the army and no one  wants to get  long covered no one wants to get covered  and pass it on somebody who becomes  seriously ill or worse dies so it's in  all our interest to carry on  using every tool that we've got to  defeat coronavirus  and the the app will remain an important  part of  of of our armory to do that and we  encourage people to carry on using it in  the same way as we encourage people to  uh keep applying common sense you know  all the things we know about better  outside than indoors and all the rest of  it and the apps is another  part of that uh of that armory  i do i do want to finish off our  discussion this morning if i can  transport secretary  on your brief and about public transport  over the past couple of days you will  have seen  a number of groups that are interested  you know perhaps representing those who  are  immunosuppressed representing people who  were in the vulnerable category before  the removal of the removal of the  mandating of mass on public transport  is petrifying vulnerable people up and  down the country  what would your advice be to those that  have to travel by public transport who  fall into that category  would you simply say that they should  travel off peak  yeah for start we'll still be issuing  guidelines and  as with everything to do with  coronavirus similar to the conversation  we're just having  um we've been living with this for a  year and a half now people know  uh the the things to do in order to try  to keep themselves um safe  and it's still sensible to wear a face  covering if you're on a a crowded piece  of transport a crowded tube for example  um but clearly if you're on a train on a  perhaps a long distance  service there's no one else in the  carriage um this is not really  protecting anyone and we're able to now  shift to people using their own common  sense  uh increasingly as we get through the  19th of july  uh and asking people to do that so i  think it's right that we switch these  things  from the law to guidance but nonetheless  that doesn't mean that we're not guiding  people to  to be sensible uh and to think about how  they can protect themselves  and others um but recognizing the fact  that  the majority of adults have now been  double vaccined and of course  reminding people to come forward for  their vaccinations and their final  vaccination if they've had the first one  i can't have a government minister on  the program without at least once  referencing the football coming up on  uh on sunday um i won't ask you for a  prediction for the  for the result but strange reports  circulating this morning about people  pushing  perhaps for a bank holiday on monday if  uh  if england do perhaps allowing people to  come in slightly later  uh for work what do you think well i  wouldn't want to jinx it i think is what  what i'd say we're we're all not in my  lifetime i don't think in yours um  have we been in this position i don't  want to say anything that's going to  make any presumptions at all and good  luck to england on  sunday we'll be right behind you

